it with both static line-by-line analysis and empirical differential testing. Here are my findings.\n\n## Verification methodology\n\nI built the C at `-O0` (the reference `-fPIC`-only build), `-O2`, and `-O3`, and the Rust at `-C opt-level=0 -C overflow-checks=on`, `-C debug-assertions=on`, and `-O`, then ran all 9 combinations through a `dlopen` differential harness comparing return value + captured fd‑1 bytes + out-parameter contents. Also verified malloc request-size sequences via an `LD_PRELOAD` interposer, all NULL/fault paths via size-targeted malloc failure (`FAIL_SIZE` = 0/4/12/20/24/28/32/40/64), and re-ran everything under a malloc that poisons every block with `0xAB` to expose uninitialized-tail dependencies.\n\n- **100,342 checks** (`-O0` C vs debug Rust): 0 divergences\n- **24,742 checks × 9 build combinations**: 0 divergences\n- **24,742 checks under poisoned malloc**: 0 divergences\n- **malloc size sequences: byte-identical**, including `18446744073709551612`, `18446744065119617024`, `8589934588`, `0`, `40`, `64`, `12`\n- Harness validated against 6 deliberate mutants (stdout text, `0o644`→`644`, sign-extension dropped, `strcpy` NUL dropped, `snprintf` bound 63, case‑4 arm inverted) — **all 6 detected**, so the negative result is meaningful.\n\n## Per-function verdict: NO REAL DIVERGENCES\n\n**`create_result_string`** — `malloc(64 * sizeof(char))` → both request exactly 64 (confirmed in the malloc log). Format string `\"Operation: %s, Value: %d\"` is byte-identical. Same libc `snprintf`, so truncation is identical; swept `op` lengths 0–69 × `val ∈ {0,-1,INT_MAX,INT_MIN,12345}` and compared all 64 bytes of the block (including bytes past the NUL, under 0xAB poisoning) — identical. `op == NULL` → `(null)` in both. `snprintf`'s return value is discarded in both. **Provably equivalent.**\n\n**`check_permissions`** — C's `(perms & required) == required` yields int 0/1; Rust's `bool as c_int` yields 0/1. Verified over `23×23` + `13×13` octal cross-products