(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\", p)], false);","+        assert_c_section_contains(&report, &[\"Mode 2: Operation: multiply, Value: 42\", \"RET=42\"]);","         assert_sections_match(&report);","+        for scenario in [\"cm1\", \"cm3\", \"cm4\", \"cm9\"] {","+            let report = run_child(scenario, 0, &[(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\", p)], false);","+            assert_sections_match(&report);","+        }","     }"," }"]}],"originalFile":"// CONFIGS.md row 44 — the whole public surface re-run on a heap that is\n// pre-filled with a non-zero pattern.\n//\n// Why this row exists: two buffers in the C are read after only a *partial*\n// write, so their tails carry whatever `malloc()` handed out —\n//\n//   * the 64-byte block from `create_result_string`, of which `snprintf` writes\n//     only the formatted prefix plus a NUL, and\n//   * `Result.operation[32]`, of which `strcpy` writes only `strlen(lit) + 1`\n//     bytes before `printf(\"Operation performed: %s\\n\", ...)` walks it.\n//\n// On a fresh heap those tails are zero-filled kernel pages, so a translation\n// bug such as a `strcpy` that forgets the NUL terminator, or an `snprintf`\n// bound that is off by one, produces *identical* output to the C and slips\n// through every other test.  Setting glibc's `MALLOC_PERTURB_` makes the tails a\n// deterministic non-zero pattern, which turns those bugs into a visible\n// difference — and the comparison stays meaningful because both libraries see\n// the same pattern.\n//\n// The sweep also hex-dumps all 64 bytes of every returned block, so the bytes\n// past the NUL are compared too, not just the C string.\n\nmod common;\n\nuse common::*;\n\n/// A few different perturb bytes: glibc fills freshly allocated memory with\n/// `byte ^ 0xff` and freed memory with `byte`, so different values exercise\n/// different tail patterns (including 0xff and 0x01 extremes).\nconst PERTURB_VALUES: &[&str] = &[\"170\", \"1\", \"255\", \"85\"];\n\n#[test]\nfn row44_full_sweep_on_perturbed_heap() {\n    for p in PERTURB_VALUES {\n        let report = run_child(\"sweep\", 0, &[(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\", p)], false);\n        // sanity: the sweep really ran the whole surface\n        assert_c_section_contains(\n            &report,\n            &[\n                \"CRS len=0 val=0:\",\n                \"CRS len=70 val=42:\",\n                \"CRS NULL:\",\n                \"MWL 6 7: RET=42\",\n                \"CM 1 6 7 8: RET=13\",\n                \"CM -1 6 7 8: RET=-1\",\n                \"CHK 420 420: 1\",\n                \"CAS 3: 21\",\n                \"CMP: 0\",\n                \"Operation performed: multiplication\",\n            ],\n        );\n        assert_sections_match(&report);\n    }\n}\n\n/// The perturbing must actually be in effect, otherwise the row above would be\n/// a no-op: with a poisoned heap the tail of `create_result_string`'s block is\n/// the fill pattern, not zeros.\n#[test]\nfn row44_control_perturbing_is_effective() {\n    let clean = run_child(\"sweep\", 0, &[(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\", \"0\")], false);\n    let dirty = run_child(\"sweep\", 0, &[(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\", \"170\")], false);\n\n    // Both runs must be internally consistent (C == Rust) ...\n    assert_sections_match(&clean);\n    assert_sections_match(&dirty);\n\n    // ... but the two runs must differ from each other, proving the tail bytes\n    // of the 64-byte block are really part of the comparison.\n    let cs = c_section(&clean);\n    let ds = c_section(&dirty);\n    assert_ne!(\n        cs, ds,\n        \"MALLOC_PERTURB_ had no observable effect — the heap-tail comparison is vacuous\"\n    );\n\n    // With perturb byte 170 the fill byte is 170 ^ 0xff = 0x55, so an empty op\n    // and val 0 must leave 0x55 padding after the NUL.\n    let line = ds\n        .lines()\n        .find(|l| l.starts_with(\"CRS len=0 val=0:\"))\n        .expect(\"sweep line missing\");\n    assert!(\n        line.contains(\"55555555\"),\n        \"expected 0x55 heap padding in {line}\"\n    );\n    // ... and on the un-perturbed run the same line ends in zero padding.\n    let line0 = cs\n        .lines()\n        .find(|l| l.starts_with(\"CRS len=0 val=0:\"))\n        .expect(\"sweep line missing\");\n    assert!(\n        line0.ends_with(\"00000000\"),\n        \"expected zero heap padding in {line0}\"\n    );\n}\n\n/// Same sweep with the heap *also* perturbed while a malloc failure is armed:\n/// the failure branches must still agree byte for byte.\n#[test]\nfn row44_perturbed_heap_with_malloc_failure() {\n    for (scenario, size, needle) in [\n        (\"crs\", 64u64, \"RET_PTR=<NULL>\"),\n        (\"mwl\", 64, \"RET=0 LOG=<NULL>\"),\n        (\"cm2\", 64, \"Log message creation failed\"),\n        (\"cm1\", 40, \"Failed to allocate result tracker\"),\n        (\"cas\", 12, \"Memory allocation failed\"),\n    ] {\n        let report = run_child(scenario, size, &[(\"MALLOC_PERTURB_\", \"170\")], true);\n        assert_c_section_contains(&report, &[needle]);\n        assert_sections_match(&report);\n    }\n}\n","userModified":false}}
