ount as isize as usize` → `count as u32 as usize` (zero- instead of sign-extension) | **all 44 pass** | pass | FAILS (caught) |\n113\t\n114\tThe first mutant is invisible without a non-zero heap because the byte after a\n115\tshort `strcpy` is already `0` on fresh pages; the last is invisible without the\n116\tsize log because both request sizes are far too large to satisfy, so both\n117\timplementations print `Memory allocation failed` and return `-1`.\n118\t\n119\t## Divergence found and fixed (release configuration)\n120\t\n121\tRunning the matrix against the **optimised** Rust build exposed a real\n122\tdivergence that the debug build could not show:\n123\t\n124\t| | C (`-O0` … `-O3`) | Rust before fix (`--release`) |\n125\t|---|---|---|\n126\t| `complexmode(3, 6, 7, 8)` while `malloc(12)` fails | `Memory allocation failed` / `Mode 3: Array Sum` / `Result: -1`, returns `-1` | `Mode 3: Array Sum` / `Result: 21`, returns `21` |\n127\t\n128\tCause: `copy_and_sum` is inlined into `complexmode`'s mode-3 arm, after which its\n129\tbuffer no longer escapes, so LLVM deleted the `malloc`/`free` pair. With the\n130\tallocation gone the `dest == NULL` branch became unreachable and the C's\n131\tallocation-failure path simply did not exist in the Rust `.so`. Row 45's\n132\trequest-size log caught it first (`SIZES cm 3: [40,12]` vs `[40]`), and the\n133\tbehavioural difference was then confirmed directly.\n134\t\n135\tFix: `src/lib.rs` routes all three `malloc()` results through\n136\t`keep_allocation()` (a `core::hint::black_box` identity function), so every\n137\tallocation — and therefore every `NULL` check the C performs — survives at all\n138\toptimisation levels. Regression test:\n139\t`error_paths.rs::row05c_copy_and_sum_allocation_failure_through_complexmode`.\n140\t\n141\tThis is why the suite is run for every configuration rather than just the\n142\tdefault: the code is identical, but the observable behaviour was not."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f77